WEBVTT

cue-VID_U1_05-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Business objectives are the aims or targets a business works towards. A new firm may put survival first. An established firm may chase profit, growth or a larger market share. Some also pursue corporate social responsibility, known as CSR. Objectives give direction, coordinate decisions and provide a yardstick for success. A useful objective is SMART: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound.

cue-VID_U1_05-2
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Wardwell Hospital Catering sets a SMART objective. Specific: improve the patient meal rating on the wards. Measurable: raise satisfaction from seventy percent to eighty-five percent. Achievable: train the kitchen team and add two menu choices. Relevant: better meals support patient recovery and the hospital contract. Time-bound: reach the target within twelve months. Because each part is clear, therefore progress can be tracked.

cue-VID_U1_05-3
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Internal stakeholders work within or own the business. At Wardwell Hospital Catering, the owners want profit and a stable contract. Managers want responsibility and bonuses. Employees want higher pay and job security. Their aims can differ: owners may cut costs while staff want better terms. Because managers sit between both groups, therefore they must balance competing demands. A stakeholder has a direct interest in the firm.

cue-VID_U1_05-4
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:44.000
External stakeholders are separate from the business but have a direct interest in it. Patients are customers who want good meals and value. Fenhay Foods, the supplier, wants reliable payment and repeat orders. The lender wants repayment. Government wants tax revenue. The local community wants jobs without noise or traffic. Because each group has a different aim, therefore a decision that helps one may harm another.

cue-VID_U1_05-5
00:01:44.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Here is a conflict map. Wardwell Hospital Catering employs forty kitchen staff. Giving each worker fifty cents more per hour for forty hours costs eight hundred dollars a week. Because staff feel valued, therefore meal quality improves and patients complain less. So the hospital renews the contract. However, profit falls by the same eight hundred dollars, so shareholders want a smaller pay rise. Watch the pressure lines pull in opposite directions around the decision.

cue-VID_U1_05-6
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Objectives change as a business matures. Wardwell Hospital Catering began with survival: winning one contract and covering costs. Once cash flow steadied, the objective shifted to growth, adding two more hospital sites. Later it targeted a larger market share. The business cycle, competition and owner values all drive change. Because priorities shift over time, therefore a SMART target set today may be replaced next year.

cue-VID_U1_05-7
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Should Wardwell Hospital Catering put corporate social responsibility ahead of short-term profit? On one side, sourcing local organic ingredients and cutting food waste builds a strong reputation. Patients and the hospital trust value it, and loyalty can win more contracts. On the other side, those ingredients cost more, so margins fall this year. Therefore CSR can support long-term growth, but a firm must first earn enough to survive. Both aims matter.

cue-VID_U1_05-8
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Final application. Wardwell Hospital Catering has a stable contract and wants to add two hospital sites next year. Which objective leads, and which stakeholder might object? Growth fits a mature firm with steady cash flow. A lender may worry about new debt, and staff may fear extra workload. Write one SMART version of that growth target. Watch the objective ladder rise while the stakeholder lines tug against it.

